course: biology
lesson: 36
title: Insects: six legs, three parts; ants, bees and wasps
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every adult insect is built on the same plan: six legs and a body in three parts, a head with eyes and feelers, a thorax where the legs and wings join, and an abdomen at the back.
explain: Let a ladybird walk across your hand: three legs touch down in a steady triangle while the other three step, then they swap.
explain: Insects wear their skeleton outside like armour, so to grow they split it and climb out, leaving an empty skin like the dragonfly nymph's by the pond, and they breathe through tiny holes along their sides.
explain: Ants, bees and wasps live in big families run by a queen: ants follow smell trails to a crumb on the patio, and a honeybee dances on the comb to tell her sisters where the flowers are.
explain: Only females sting, and a honeybee that stings a person usually dies, so the wasp at the picnic is a far better reason to stay calm than to swat.
fact: Every adult insect has six legs and a body in three parts: head, thorax and abdomen; the legs and wings are all fixed to the thorax.
fact: Insects have a hard outside skeleton called an exoskeleton, and to grow they moult, splitting it and climbing out in a new soft one.
fact: Insects have no lungs: air enters through little holes along the sides called spiracles and travels down branching tubes to every part, which only works well over short distances, one reason insects stay small.
fact: A scout ant leaves a smell trail from food to the nest; others follow and strengthen it while the food lasts, then the trail fades.
fact: Leafcutter ants do not eat the leaves they carry: they chew them to grow a fungus garden underground and eat the fungus.
fact: Many ants can lift 10 to 50 times their own weight, because small animals are strong for their size; an ant blown up to the size of a person could hardly hold up its own weight.
fact: A honeybee's waggle dance shows the direction of flowers compared with the sun and, by the length of the waggle, how far away they are.
fact: Bees make honey from nectar by passing it between workers and fanning it until most of the water is gone; one worker makes only about a twelfth of a teaspoon in her life, and honey left too watery can go mouldy.
fact: Only female bees and wasps sting; a honeybee's barbed sting stays in human skin and the bee dies, while a wasp's smooth sting slides out, so it can sting many times.
fact: Wasps build nests of paper by chewing wood and mixing it with spit, in six-sided cells, which fit together with no gaps and use the least material.
fact: A ladybird's number of spots shows which kind it is, such as the 7-spot or the 2-spot, not how old it is.
fact: A honeybee colony has one queen laying up to about 1,500 eggs a day in summer, tens of thousands of female workers and some male drones.
fact: Bumblebees warm up by shivering their flight muscles, so they can fly on cold spring mornings when honeybees cannot.
fact: The story that science says bumblebees cannot fly is a myth from a rough sum that treated a flapping wing like a stiff aeroplane wing.
exercise: How many legs does an adult insect have? | Six | Eight | Four | Ten
exercise: What are the three parts of an insect's body? | Head, thorax and abdomen | Head, tail and a pair of wings | Head, neck and the six legs | Head, chest and back legs
exercise: Where are all six of an insect's legs fixed? | On the thorax, the middle part | Two on each body part | On the abdomen, the back part | Under the head
exercise: Why does an insect leave an empty skin behind, like the dragonfly nymph on a reed? | Its outside skeleton cannot stretch, so it moults to grow | It is shedding skin to cool down | It was eaten by a bird or a frog and only its empty skin was left | It changes its skin every single night
exercise: What are the tiny dots along the side of a caterpillar? | Spiracles, holes it breathes through | Its eyes | Its ears | Spots of paint that rubbed off from the leaves
exercise: How do ants find their way to a biscuit crumb? | They follow a smell trail left by a scout ant | They can see the crumb from far away with their big eyes | They hear the crumb fall | They follow the sun to it
exercise: What do leafcutter ants do with the leaves they carry? | Grow a fungus garden on them and eat the fungus | Eat the leaves straight away | Use them as umbrellas to keep off the rain and the sun | Build walls for their nest
exercise: About how much can many ants lift? | 10 to 50 times their own weight | Only about half their own weight | Exactly their own weight, no more | A thousand times their own weight, like lifting a car
exercise: What does a honeybee's waggle dance tell the other bees? | The direction and distance of good flowers | That the queen is hungry | That it is time to sleep | Which of the bees must leave the hive today and never come back
exercise: How do bees turn nectar into honey? | They pass it between them and fan it until most water is gone | They boil it with their body heat | They mix it with yellow pollen and chew it into a thick paste for weeks | They leave it in the sun to dry
exercise: About how much honey does one worker bee make in her whole life? | About a twelfth of a teaspoon | About a jar | About a cup | About a bucket, full to the top
exercise: Which bees and wasps can sting? | Only the females | Only the male bees | All of them, males too | Only the queens, and no one else
exercise: What happens to a honeybee that stings a person? | Its barbed sting stays in the skin and the bee dies | It flies off and goes straight on to sting someone else in the garden | It loses its wings but lives | Nothing happens to it
exercise: What is a wasp nest made of? | Paper made from chewed wood and spit | Wax made in the wasps' bodies, like bees | Mud and small stones | Leaves and grass
exercise: Who lays all the eggs in a honeybee colony? | The queen | The drones | Every worker in turn | The beekeeper adds them
exercise: How does a bumblebee fly on a chilly spring morning? | It shivers its flight muscles to warm up | It waits for a big honeybee to come and carry it on its back | It drinks hot nectar | It cannot fly until noon
test: lol science says bumblebees shouldn't be able to fly. mad right? | Myth: the sum used a stiff aeroplane wing, but bee wings flap and twist | True, nobody knows how they fly | True, they float along on clouds of pollen | True, they fly by magic
test: My friend says ladybird spots tell you its age, 7 spots means 7 years. Right? | No, the number of spots shows which kind of ladybird it is | Yes, one spot grows each year | Yes, but only for red ones | No, the spots show how many eggs it laid over the summer, one for each egg
test: You wipe your finger across a line of ants on the patio. What happens? | They get confused and wander until they find the smell again | They all march straight on | They bite your finger and die | They all turn round and make a brand new line in another place at once
test: The biscuit crumb has all been carried away. What will the ant trail do? | It will fade as ants stop adding smell | It will last forever | It will get stronger | It will move to another biscuit by itself
test: In a cartoon a giant ant the size of a man lifts a bus. What would really happen to an ant that big? | Its legs could hardly hold it up | It could lift a whole city | It would be exactly as strong as before | It would fly away
test: Why do insects stay so small today? | Air moving through tubes only works over short distances | They do not eat enough | Birds keep them small | They stop growing on purpose, so that birds will not see them in the grass
test: A wasp lands on your jam sandwich. bloody thing! What should you do? | Stay calm and move away, don't swat | Swat it hard with your hand or a book | Shout loudly and wave your arms about at it | Blow hard on it to scare it away from you
test: Why does a honeybee die after stinging you but a wasp does not? | The bee's sting has barbs that stay stuck; the wasp's is smooth | Wasps keep spare stings in their legs, so they never run out | Bees are allergic to people | Wasps never sting people
test: What would happen if all the worker bees in a hive stopped fanning the nectar? | The honey would stay watery and could go mouldy | It would turn into wax | It would become stronger honey | The queen would have to make all the honey herself instead
test: Why are wasp nest cells six-sided? | Six-sided cells fit with no gaps and use the least material | Wasps have six legs | Round cells are much too hard for wasps to make with their jaws | They copy them from snowflakes
test: A beekeeper lifts a frame crawling with bees. Most of them are what? | Female workers | The male drones | The queens of the hive | Baby bees in the cells
test: In a game your bee hero is a male worker who stings enemies. What's wrong? | Workers are female, and males cannot sting | Nothing, male workers sting very well | Bees never sting anything | Only queens can fly
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the body plan of an adult insect? | Six legs and a body of three parts, the legs being attached to the thorax | Eight legs and a body of two parts | Six legs distributed two to each body part | Four legs and a pair of feelers, with wings upon the head and abdomen
exam: Identify the principal function of the honeybee's waggle dance. | To communicate the direction and distance of a food source | To summon the colony to sleep at dusk | To expel drones from the hive | To warm the brood
exam: Ascertain the use made by leafcutter ants of the foliage which they harvest. | The cultivation of a fungus upon which they feed | Direct consumption as food | The roofing of their nest | The wrapping and protection of their eggs during the cold season
exam: It is given that a honeybee stings a human being. Determine the consequence for the bee. | The barbed sting remains lodged and the bee dies | The bee is unharmed and may sting again | The bee loses its wings only and must walk back to the hive | The bee becomes a queen
